```json
// v1
{
  "type": "record",
  "name": "OrderPlaced",
  "fields": [
    {"name": "orderId", "type": "string"},
    {"name": "amount", "type": "double"}
  ]
}

// v2 — safe: new field has a default
{
  "type": "record",
  "name": "OrderPlaced",
  "fields": [
    {"name": "orderId", "type": "string"},
    {"name": "amount", "type": "double"},
    {"name": "currency", "type": "string", "default": "USD"}
  ]
}
```

The `default` is what makes this safe: a consumer still on v1 code ignores the extra `currency` field entirely, and a consumer already upgraded to v2 reading an *old* v1 message gets `"USD"` filled in automatically instead of an error. Drop the `default` and the same field addition becomes a breaking change for exactly the second case — v2 code has no way to fill in a value the old message never had.
